#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b15598d0-a300-5067-80a0-4f58a33bfcfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187543} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f5f5cd-2725-5b56-bf55-6d1ac9c5771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187544} {:answer/id #uuid "1a629bb9-2d45-5781-b023-9d727557486c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187545} {:answer/id #uuid "24104593-8a31-518d-b3ab-dedc0c1ac151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187546} {:answer/id #uuid "36af65ed-cc42-5b73-9282-fda21d2e073e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187547}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ffcaf15a-b093-5793-9500-03e1ad502daa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187542} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80413058-7a42-50ce-b340-c38037f5e452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366034} {:answer/id #uuid "88e1d287-8927-58d8-8cc3-0f5341148e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366035} {:answer/id #uuid "56750f1c-d37b-5ed0-95b2-543990047e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366036} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c88594-75cf-5584-aa94-856988c0b499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366037} {:answer/id #uuid "36e38e8d-e602-5755-9e6f-06535d1903bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36e38e8d-e602-5755-9e6f-06535d1903bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366038} :answer-field/id #uuid "609eb989-9967-575f-a39a-2755b35e41be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e86ac40c-9f96-593f-88c6-aef65bbc3b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-38136" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=1$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n- $T$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9073a4d0-22ed-5cf8-af81-c3296508e37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191866} {:answer/id #uuid "c2098c3f-878f-51d2-87b8-dcb3ce8b7369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191867} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ee86a3-e19e-50f4-874f-c5daecff016c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191868} {:answer/id #uuid "fca4e297-448b-5c39-a9c9-5315475f554d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191869} {:answer/id #uuid "79060e09-a9fe-5e9b-abf1-38113fbe5e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191870}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3287ea6e-a378-594b-9a2c-67f15921ba3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de3b48ac-ad74-5cf1-9366-c0b84cab1fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379780} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb1bef2-5cce-573a-bf79-bbfbc33b724f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379781} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc0500b-e5e9-541c-8388-24405da1e57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379782} {:answer/id #uuid "3bde1763-c17f-5905-bbb1-e2a6e02b57e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379783} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2a1b06-67db-5221-afa2-7618ffd9d26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb1bef2-5cce-573a-bf79-bbfbc33b724f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379781} :answer-field/id #uuid "0352c60c-3be5-56be-b959-d8ac481c11cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8166eb05-ff92-507f-8eb2-6ecb8cda44b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-35866" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x<2,\\,0<y<2}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2,}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,-1\\le x\\le 1}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5ec986a-7949-52e5-9ea3-e6dd3de167de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194328} {:answer/id #uuid "c67a1dfb-2ecb-54c5-892a-b9e7b4c1e113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194329} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dbfa9e-6ca8-5450-a467-361235f8ae41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194330} {:answer/id #uuid "0316a894-45c1-52d6-8644-136d48f31984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194331} {:answer/id #uuid "552e0732-9cdf-562b-bfc8-69721f75199e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194332}] :answer-field/id #uuid "026499be-9d1e-50a3-aefd-54538795e249" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194327} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa4ba54-fb10-540b-913c-c22d6d09ecd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383536} {:answer/id #uuid "dca5c6b4-b9a4-5099-a402-337421f6c7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383537} {:answer/id #uuid "138dde2d-8a1f-5d25-be3d-d149af8abc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383538} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe43dcc-4d57-5e39-b3e6-11c357696049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383539} {:answer/id #uuid "5b168751-e45c-5e8c-83ec-81be6a54e6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b168751-e45c-5e8c-83ec-81be6a54e6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383540} :answer-field/id #uuid "164ed81b-856e-50e1-9161-0818ce673900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7c80401-6b6e-5da5-9047-45e3ded9ecb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161828" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible on $U.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=-{f}^{′}(a).$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$ which could be smaller than $U.$ \n\nFor example, the function $f(x)={x}^{2}$ satisfies the conditions of the inverse function theorem for $U=(-2,2)$ and $a=1.$ However, $f(x)$ is not invertible on the entire set $U,$ only on a subset of $U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb954d41-7aec-50ff-9088-73366520bd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228113} {:answer/id #uuid "e878630a-75fc-5919-9018-2b107367a072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228114} {:answer/id #uuid "20a73ed5-b452-545c-a27d-31335b998f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228115} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3e450d-45fa-58ed-b194-fc735133412c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228116} {:answer/id #uuid "650f038e-f1e1-5ea6-9620-dcf6fe0029da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "650f038e-f1e1-5ea6-9620-dcf6fe0029da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228117} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc0de205-3871-5589-99ce-a25b0449ebb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d6b24a2-a25a-5fce-957a-e0f07d99344e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333079" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-4Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.3.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)((1-4Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-4Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-4Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-4Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-4Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007def61-c0db-5afc-857c-bb1794f44c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420498} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac9e6ca-aa28-5456-80e0-6b2c6eed18c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420499} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4787e2-6983-5165-b71f-60ddbc84d9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420500} {:answer/id #uuid "89c37704-a55e-55ef-8110-12970ee2c9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420501} {:answer/id #uuid "e9eece08-2814-52b9-9cbf-85f258fc3528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c4787e2-6983-5165-b71f-60ddbc84d9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420500} :answer-field/id #uuid "236e449f-8bdf-5cb7-898e-9834bb6e7cf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420503}] :question/id #uuid "d1a7556c-36af-5f0b-b64c-a00089e6850e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37456" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=2\\cdot 0-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=2>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ then $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
